   {
    "text": "In the quiet halls of ancient stone, where shadows dance and light is cast,  \nThe church has stood through centuries, a beacon of faith and grace.  \nBut beneath its walls, a quiet change has taken root—  \nA shift in how the sacred was shared, and how it was taught.\n\nThe first reductions came with time, a humble act of grace,  \nA way to make the Gospel more accessible, more real.  \nThey trimmed the edges of the liturgy, softened the tone,  \nSo that the faithful could come, not just to hear, but to feel.\n\nAnd so the fiesta of the Mass became a simpler song,  \nA feast of bread and wine, not just of bread and wine.  \nThe priest no longer wore the full vestments, but still spoke with truth,  \nAnd the people gathered, not just to listen, but to be heard.\n\nThen came the reductions, a movement that spread like fire,  \nA call to make the sacred more present, more near.  \nThey cut the weight of tradition, let the message flow,  \nSo that the grace of Christ could be felt, not just seen.\n\nAnd in the parish halls, the people gathered, not just to hear,  \nBut to feel the love that was made, and the love that was given.  \nThe fiesta of the Mass became a celebration of life,  \nA time to share, to give, to receive, to be alive.\n\nSo here we stand, with the history of reductions in our hearts,  \nA legacy of love, of grace, of faith, of light.  \nThe church has changed, but the message remains the same—  \nA fiesta of the soul, a fiesta of the heart, a fiesta of the flame.",
